Self-Harm
Young Adult & Adolecence
Unexplained superficial lacerations/burns, Scars in varying healing stages, Concealment behaviours

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-037
Reason for Visit: Acute medical assessment of intentional soft tissue lacerations to determine the need for suturing, combined with an urgent psychiatric risk assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a highly distressed patient who had engaged in acute self-injurious behavior (cutting) following a period of severe emotional dysregulation. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critically required to evaluate the depth of the lacerations, exclude major vascular or tendinous injury requiring surgical repair, and perform an immediate psychiatric safety assessment to prevent further, potentially fatal, self-harm prior to morning mental health services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I couldn't stop the pressure in my head, I felt completely numb, and cutting was the only way to release the tension."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents following an acute episode of self-harm, consisting of multiple linear superficial lacerations on the volar aspect of the left forearm, inflicted with a razor blade approximately 1 hour ago. The patient describes experiencing expressions of intense emotional distress and dissociation preceding the act, followed by an immediate, maladaptive relief of psychological tension following the self-injury. The flatmate discovered the patient attempting to conceal the injuries by quickly putting on a long-sleeved shirt despite the warm weather. The patient admits to a history of frequent unexplained 'accidents' and increasing social withdrawal and isolation over the past two months. Co-morbid signs of a severe depressive episode are prominent.
Functional Status: Hemodynamically stable; currently sitting quietly on the bed, appearing emotionally exhausted and blunted.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute psychiatric risk; flatmate is highly anxious and unsure of how to maintain a safe environment.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Patient makes poor eye contact, speaks in a monotone voice, and is actively holding a blood-soaked towel to the left forearm.
Physical Exam: Physical inspection of the left forearm reveals 6 fresh, linear lacerations. The wounds are superficial, breaching the epidermis and superficial dermis, but definitively not involving the deep fascia, underlying musculature, tendons, or major neurovascular structures. Capillary refill in the digits is normal (<2 seconds), and distal sensation/motor function is fully intact. Crucially, the examination reveals the presence of multiple scars in various stages of healing adjacent to the fresh wounds. The clinical presentation perfectly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of unexplained superficial lacerations, scars in varying healing stages, and active concealment behaviours.
Environmental Observations: A disassembled shaving razor is visible on the bathroom sink; the flatmate has already removed other obvious sharps from the immediate vicinity.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Non-Suicidal Self-Injury (Superficial Lacerations) with underlying Severe Emotional Dysregulation - Status: Acute Crisis
Differentials:

Accidental Trauma - Ruled out/unlikely due to the uniform, parallel, linear nature of the lacerations, the presence of older scars in identical patterns, and the patient's explicit admission of intent.

Arterial/Major Venous Laceration - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of pulsatile bleeding, rapidly expanding hematoma, or signs of distal ischemia, confirming the wounds are superficial.

Psychotic Episode - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient is fully oriented, denies auditory/visual hallucinations commanding the self-harm, and the act was driven by a desire to relieve emotional tension rather than a psychotic delusion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Requires continuous supervision and environmental lockdown)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient engaged in an acute episode of non-suicidal self-injury driven by severe emotional distress and dissociation. The presence of fresh, bleeding lacerations required an immediate after-hours physical evaluation to rule out deep structural damage that would necessitate emergency surgical intervention. Physical examination safely confirmed the wounds were superficial and amenable to conservative closure. More critically, the urgent visit facilitated an immediate psychiatric safety assessment. While the patient lacked an active plan for suicide, the high level of distress and access to lethal means required the immediate implementation of a strict environmental safety plan with the flatmate to prevent escalation overnight, an intervention that could not wait for routine daytime hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Thoroughly irrigated the lacerations with sterile saline. Applied Steri-Strips (tissue adhesive strips) to approximate the wound edges, followed by a sterile compressive dressing. Conducted a comprehensive safety and risk assessment. Mandated immediate environmental lockdown by the flatmate (securing all remaining sharps, medications, and household chemicals).
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage overwhelming emotional distress without tissue damage, hold an ice cube tightly in the hand until it melts, utilizing the intense but harmless biological sensation of cold to ground the nervous system and replace the urge to cut. To reduce the immediate risk of secondary bacterial infection, leave the sterile dressing entirely intact and dry for 48 hours, establishing a routine of physical self-care that directly counteracts the self-injurious impulse.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral sent to the local Acute Psychiatric Triage team for review tomorrow morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Basic wound care supplies provided from the clinical bag.
Follow-up: Mandatory, urgent review by the regular GP tomorrow morning to assess wound healing and initiate a formal Mental Health Treatment Plan and psychological evaluation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient expresses an active intent to end their life, attempts further self-harm, or if the flatmate feels they can no longer guarantee the patient's safety, they must immediately drive the patient to the Emergency Department or call 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical wounds have been treated and are safe; however, the emotional distress driving this behavior requires urgent, specialized psychological support.
Clinical Course: The superficial lacerations will heal within 7 to 10 days if kept clean; the mental health team will contact you tomorrow to begin addressing the underlying emotional pain.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Tell your flatmate instantly if the feelings of tension return and you feel the urge to hurt yourself again tonight. Flatmate: Do not leave the patient unsupervised at any point until they are reviewed by the medical team tomorrow.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The cuts are shallow and have been safely closed with sterile strips to stop the bleeding and prevent infection. The urge to self-harm is a sign that your brain is overwhelmed by emotional distress and is using physical pain as a maladaptive release valve. This is a recognized medical issue that requires psychological therapy to develop healthier coping mechanisms.
Actionable Steps: Allow your flatmate to secure all sharp objects and medications in the house; this is a standard medical protocol to keep you safe tonight. Keep the bandage completely dry and do not peel off the strips. Try the ice-cube technique if the intense tension returns.

